Contract & Scope Risk Agent: Design Draft
v1
Gate: GI Solution Assurance | Status: first draft for leadership review | Format: hackathon
build
1. Problem
Many delivery problems start in the paperwork. Statements of Work (SOWs) are vague,
assumptions are never written down, and contract clauses commit the team to
obligations nobody planned for. The team usually finds out around 50% into the project,
through scope disputes, unplanned effort and pricing gaps. By then, fixing it means
change requests, margin erosion and strained client relationships.
Today, catching these issues depends on a delivery manager and legal reading long
documents under time pressure. It is slow, inconsistent, and overloads both groups.
Core need: an agent that reads the SOW and supporting documents before approval and
returns a severity-ranked list of gaps, with a risk score, so issues are fixed before signing,
not after project start.
2. Goals and non-goals
Goals
Identify what we are committing to deliver, including obligations buried in small print.
Check whether assumptions are written, and flag the ones that are missing.
Judge whether scope is vague or broader than it looks.
Surface commercial rights and risks: penalties, liability, payment terms, changerequest mechanism.
Detect legal conflicts between documents and deviations from internal policy.
Check resource numbers (roles, headcount, effort) against the scope.
Rank every finding by severity and give an overall risk score with a sign / negotiate /
escalate recommendation.
Reduce the review burden on legal and delivery managers.
Non-goals (for the hackathon)
Replacing legal review or giving legal advice. The agent supports a human decision.
