3. Users
Delivery manager (primary): uploads the SOW and supporting documents, gets the
report, and fixes most issues directly.
Legal: receives only Critical and legal-conflict items.
Commercial and finance leader: views payment, penalty and exposure findings.
TPM: views scope, assumption and resourcing findings.
4. Tech stack
LLM: Claude, using structured (JSON) output so every finding follows a fixed schema.
Use a stronger model for analysis and ranking, and a lighter one for parsing and
extraction if cost or latency matters.
Orchestration: plain Python to start; LangGraph if a visible agent graph is useful for
the demo.
Document parsing: PyMuPDF and python-docx; Docling if PDFs or tables are messy.
Schema and validation: Pydantic models for findings, rules and the report.
Retrieval: policy rules are few, so pass the relevant ones directly in the prompt. Use a
lightweight vector index (Chroma or FAISS) for the past-issues library.
UI: Streamlit (upload, progress view, report, export).
Export: DOCX, PDF or CSV of the risk register.
5. Architecture (first draft)
Flow: upload documents, parse, run specialist analysts, retrieve past lessons, verify, rank
and score, then render the report.
Components
1. Document Parser. Converts SOW, contract and proposal into clauses, tables and
metadata (parties, term, value, dates, payment terms). Keeps document name and
clause location so every finding can cite its source.
