2. Scope, Assumption and Resource Analyst. Looks for vague verbs and quantifiers,
missing acceptance criteria, no out-of-scope list, open-ended effort, missing or
unrealistic assumptions, and resource numbers that do not match the scope.
Compares the proposal against the SOW to catch promises the SOW does not bound.
3. Obligation, Commercial and Legal Analyst. Extracts every binding obligation (shall,
must, at our cost), annexure terms, SLAs and penalties, liability and indemnity, autorenewal, IP, data and audit terms, payment and milestone alignment, and the changerequest mechanism. Flags contradictions between documents and precedence
issues. Checks clauses against internal legal and general policy.
4. Historical Lessons Agent. For each candidate finding, retrieves similar clause
patterns from past contracts and the delivery issues they caused, so severity is tied
to real consequences.
5. Ranker and Critic. Verifies that each quoted clause actually exists in the source,
removes duplicates, checks severity is justified, computes scores, and writes the final
report. Findings that fail verification are dropped or re-run.
Orchestration notes
Analysts 2 and 3 can share one prompt template with different checklists.
Gaps the agent cannot resolve become a "questions to resolve before signing" list
instead of guesses.
Human in the loop: the delivery manager can accept, dismiss or escalate each finding.
Finding schema (draft fields): id, category, severity, clause quote, document and location,
why it is risky, suggested fix or clarifying question, owner (delivery, legal, commercial,
finance, TPM), policy rule reference, past-issue reference, confidence.
Severity and scoring
Tiers: Critical, High, Medium, Low.
Severity is derived from impact (financial, delivery, legal, reversibility) and likelihood,
with the weights written down so the score is explainable.
Overall Contract Risk Score out of 100, with sub-scores for Obligations, Assumptions,
Scope, Commercial, Legal and Resourcing.
Recommendation: Ready to sign, Negotiate first, or Escalate to legal.
Escalation routing: only Critical and legal-conflict findings go to legal. Everything else
stays with the delivery manager. This is how the burden on legal is reduced.
