Severity agreement: how often the agent's severity matches the reviewers' (within
one tier).
Citation accuracy: share of findings whose quoted clause exists and supports the
claim (target near 100%).
Time saved: manual review time versus agent time per document.
Burden reduction: share of findings resolved by the delivery manager without legal
involvement.
Reviewer trust: a short rating from a delivery manager and a legal reviewer on
usefulness and clarity.
Method
Run the agent on the test set, compare to the answer key, and report per-category
results.
Have a legal or delivery reviewer grade a sample of findings blind.
Back-test on a past contract that caused a delivery issue: would the agent have
flagged it?
8. Risks to the build
Missed or hallucinated clauses: mitigated by the critic step and mandatory quotes.
Over-flagging, which causes alert fatigue: mitigated by severity thresholds and deduplication.
Scanned or badly formatted PDFs: mitigated by a parsing fallback and sample-file
checks early.
Policy that is hard to codify: mitigated by starting with the highest-impact rules only.
Confidentiality of contract data: mitigated by approved environment and anonymised
demo data.
9. Questions to ask the leader before we start building
Scope and success
1. What does success look like for this challenge: catching more issues, saving review
time, or fewer disputes later? Which one is judged first?
2. Which contract types are in scope (new SOWs only, or also change requests,
renewals, MSAs)?
